records/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-07-05z-bls-ces-nonfarm-payrolls-change-2026-07/codex_events.jsonl:40:{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_20", "type": "agent_message", "text": "{\"slug\":\"nonfarm-payrolls-july-2026\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"US nonfarm payrolls, Jul 2026\",\"question\":\"What will be the first-published seasonally adjusted over-the-month change in U.S. total nonfarm payroll employment for July 2026, in thousands, in the BLS Employment Situation release?\",\"unit\":\"thousands\",\"pointEstimate\":85,\"ciLow\":-65,\"ciHigh\":235,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-08-07\",\"resolutionSource\":\"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Employment Situation (Current Employment Statistics)\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolves to the first-published seasonally adjusted over-the-month change in total nonfarm payroll employment for July 2026 stated in the BLS Employment Situation news release scheduled for August 7, 2026. Use the BLS first print in thousands as released; later monthly revisions, annual benchmark revisions, and subsequent table updates do not change the resolved value.\",\"dataPointId\":\"bls.ces.total_nonfarm_payroll_change.july_2026.first_print\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"June 2026 first print payroll change, thousands\",\"value\":57},{\"label\":\"May 2026 first print payroll change, thousands\",\"value\":172},{\"label\":\"April 2026 first print payroll change, thousands\",\"value\":115},{\"label\":\"March 2026 first print payroll change, thousands\",\"value\":178},{\"label\":\"February 2026 first print payroll change, thousands\",\"value\":-92}],\"drivers\":[\"June print was soft but not a collapse\",\"recent first prints average near moderate job growth\",\"claims remain low by recent standards\",\"health care and social assistance provide steady payroll support\",\"leisure and hospitality June weakness could partly reverse\",\"prior-month downward revisions warn against over-weighting spring strength\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://www.bls.gov/schedule/news_release/empsit.htm\",\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm\",\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.b.htm\",\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_06052026.htm\",\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_04032026.htm\",\"https://www.dol.gov/ui/data.pdf\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-07-04T19:07:32Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"July 2026 first-print nonfarm payroll forecast\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The resolver is the first BLS Employment Situation print for July 2026 total nonfarm payroll employment, seasonally adjusted, measured as the over-the-month change in thousands. I found no local registered July ledger row; I kept the canonical requested slug and used the existing BLS payroll ledger pattern for the dataPointId and first-print resolver.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the official BLS Employment Situation release calendar for the July 2026 reference month.\",\"result\":\"BLS lists July 2026 Employment Situation release on Aug. 07, 2026 at 08:30 AM; the same schedule lists June 2026 on Jul. 02, 2026 and May 2026 on Jun. 05, 2026.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Read the current BLS Employment Situation Summary for June 2026 payroll level, unemployment, revisions, and next-release notice.\",\"result\":\"The June 2026 release reported total nonfarm payroll employment +57,000, unemployment rate 4.2 percent, prior-12-month average payroll change +36,000, April revised from +179,000 to +148,000, May revised from +172,000 to +129,000, and the July 2026 release scheduled for Aug. 07, 2026.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Read BLS Summary table B for recent establishment-survey composition.\",\"result\":\"Summary table B shows total nonfarm changes of +148,000 in April 2026, +129,000 in May 2026, and +57,000 in June 2026; the 3-month average was +111,000; June sector changes included professional and business services +36,000, private education and health services +69,000, leisure and hospitality -61,000, and government +8,000.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Read BLS archived Employment Situation releases for recent first-print payroll reference points.\",\"result\":\"The May 2026 first print was +172,000; the May release stated April had first printed at +115,000 before revision to +179,000; the March 2026 release first printed March at +178,000 and February at -133,000 after February had first printed at -92,000.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Read the latest official DOL unemployment insurance weekly claims release for near-term layoff pressure.\",\"result\":\"For the week ending June 27, 2026, seasonally adjusted initial claims were 215,000, down 1,000 from 216,000; the 4-week average was 222,000; insured unemployment for the week ending June 20 was 1,814,000 and the insured unemployment rate was 1.2 percent.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: the recent first-print payroll sample of February through June is -92, +178, +115, +172, and +57 thousand, averaging about +86 thousand. The current BLS revised-data frame is softer, with +36 thousand over the prior 12 months but +111 thousand over the latest three revised months.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Level, momentum, and one-off split: June's +57 thousand print and downward revisions argue for a lower center than the spring first prints. The claims data do not show a layoff break, and the June leisure and hospitality -61 thousand looks like a candidate for partial reversal, so I do not push the center toward zero or negative payroll growth.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Policy-mechanism and sector split: health care, social assistance, and some professional services remain the reliable upside channels, while restrictive rates, slower consumer demand, weak participation, and government-sector cooling limit acceleration. July seasonal payroll measurement can also be noisy around education, hospitality, and temporary-help hiring.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Prior/update/interval: prior model is a blended persistence prior using the recent official first-print sample from February-June 2026 and the BLS current-release 12-month and 3-month reference averages. I start near the +86 thousand first-print average, subtract about 20 thousand for June softness and the -74 thousand combined April-May revision signal, add about 15 thousand for likely partial leisure/hospitality rebound and low claims, and round to +85 thousand. The 80% interval uses realized recent first-print dispersion of roughly 100 thousand, widened for July seasonal risk and skewed slightly upward for rebound risk, giving -65 to +235 thousand.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Point calculation: 86 - 20 + 15 = 81, rounded to 85 thousand to avoid false precision. Interval calculation: center 85 with a lower half-width of 150 and upper half-width of 150 gives -65 to 235 thousand, calibrated wider than one recent standard deviation because first prints can swing sharply and July seasonal factors add noise.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration and scenarios: downside outside the interval would require a broader hiring freeze, a larger leisure or retail drop, or an abrupt claims deterioration pushing payrolls below -65 thousand. Upside outside the interval would require a strong rebound from June's leisure weakness plus continued health-care and professional-services gains above 235 thousand. The central case is modest positive hiring, slower than spring but not recessionary.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":85,\"ciLow\":-65,\"ciHigh\":235}]}"}}
