orecast-examples/june-2026-wave.ts:1090:    \"question\": \"What will the seasonally adjusted month-over-month percent change in the core (excluding food and energy) PCE price index be for May 2026, as reported in the first BEA Personal Income and Outlays release on 2026-06-25?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/june-2026-wave.ts:1099:    \"resolutionRule\": \"Resolves to the month-over-month percent change in the PCE price index excluding food and energy (core PCE), seasonally adjusted, for May 2026, as printed in the first BEA Personal Income and Outlays release on 2026-06-25 at 8:30 AM ET, rounded to one decimal place at BEA's reported precision (the headline table reports to 0.1; FRED PCEPILFE supports 0.01). FRED series PCEPILFE (first vintage).\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/june-2026-wave.ts:1135:      \"BEA's first print rounds to one decimal in the headline; FRED PCEPILFE gives two-decimal precision.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/june-2026-wave.ts:1521:    \"question\": \"What will be the first-published seasonally adjusted U.S. civilian unemployment rate (U-3) for June 2026, in percent, in the BLS Employment Situation release?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/june-2026-wave.ts:1530:    \"resolutionRule\": \"Resolves to the first-published seasonally adjusted civilian unemployment rate (U-3) for June 2026 stated in the BLS Employment Situation news release scheduled for July 2, 2026. Later revisions do not change the resolved value.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:190:        \"result\": \"Fetched labor-market slack context: seasonally adjusted insured unemployment for week ending June 20 was 1,814,000, up 2,000 from the revised prior week; the insured unemployment rate was 1.2 percent; the insured-unemployment four-week average was 1,803,000, up 10,750.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:196:        \"result\": \"Fetched BLS context: total nonfarm payroll employment changed +57,000 in June 2026, the unemployment rate was 4.2 percent, leisure and hospitality employment declined by 61,000, and April-May payrolls were revised down by 74,000 combined.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:266:      \"May unemployment rate fell 0.3 percentage points to 6.6%\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:410:        \"result\": \"The May 2026 LFS reports employment of 21,122,000, employment up 88,000, unemployment rate 6.6%, unemployment rate down 0.3 percentage points, and employment had declined 112,000 over the first 4 months of 2026 before May.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:452:    \"title\": \"Australia unemployment rate, June 2026\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:453:    \"question\": \"What will the Australian Bureau of Statistics first report as Australia's seasonally adjusted unemployment rate for June 2026 in Labour Force, Australia?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:462:    \"resolutionRule\": \"Resolve to the first published seasonally adjusted unemployment rate for June 2026 in ABS Labour Force, Australia, reported in percent to one decimal place. Later revisions do not change the resolved value.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:465:        \"label\": \"Mar 2026 SA unemployment rate\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:469:        \"label\": \"Apr 2026 SA unemployment rate\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:473:        \"label\": \"May 2026 SA unemployment rate\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:477:        \"label\": \"May 2026 trend unemployment rate\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:486:      \"May unemployment rate fell to 4.4 percent after April's 4.5 percent spike\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:487:      \"Trend unemployment rate rose marginally to 4.4 percent\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:606:        \"text\": \"The target is the ABS Labour Force, Australia first-print seasonally adjusted unemployment rate for reference month June 2026, in percent to one decimal place. The ledger target is retained; the source hint mentioning CPI indicator pages appears inconsistent with the unemployment-rate series, so I used ABS Labour Force sources for evidence and resolver identity.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:618:        \"result\": \"Fetched seasonally adjusted Apr-26 unemployment rate 4.5%, May-26 unemployment rate 4.4%, unemployed people 689,600 to 671,300, employment 14,698,500 to 14,738,800, participation rate 66.6% to 66.7%, monthly hours 2,032 million to 2,010 million; trend unemployment rate 4.3% to 4.4%.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:624:        \"result\": \"Fetched seasonally adjusted unemployment rates: Jan-26 4.1%, Feb-26 4.3%, Mar-26 4.3%, Apr-26 4.5%, May-26 4.4%; trend rates Jan-26 4.3%, Feb-26 4.3%, Mar-26 4.3%, Apr-26 4.3%, May-26 4.4%.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:898:    \"title\": \"Euro area unemployment rate, June 2026\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:899:    \"question\": \"What will Eurostat first publish as the euro area seasonally adjusted unemployment rate for June 2026, total sex, age 15-74, in percent of the labour force, rounded to one decimal?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:906:    \"resolutionSource\": \"Eurostat monthly unemployment euro-indicators release / une_rt_m data page\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:908:    \"resolutionRule\": \"Resolves to the first Eurostat-published June 2026 euro area seasonally adjusted unemployment rate in dataset une_rt_m or the corresponding euro-indicators monthly unemployment release, total sex, age 15-74, unit percent of active population, rounded to one decimal. Use the first official print only; later revisions to June 2026 do not change the resolved value.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:911:        \"label\": \"2026-05 euro area unemployment rate\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:915:        \"label\": \"2026-04 euro area unemployment rate\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:919:        \"label\": \"2026-03 euro area unemployment rate\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:923:        \"label\": \"2026-02 euro area unemployment rate\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:927:        \"label\": \"2025-05 euro area unemployment rate\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:938:    \"dataPointId\": \"eurostat.unemployment_rate.euro_area.june_2026.first_print\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1049:        \"text\": \"Euro area unemployment rate, June 2026 first print\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1053:        \"text\": \"The target is Eurostat's first official June 2026 euro area seasonally adjusted unemployment rate, total sex and age 15-74, in percent of the labour force. The May 2026 Eurostat unemployment release is dated 2 July 2026 and states the next release is 30 July 2026, so I use 2026-07-30 as the verified resolution date.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1059:        \"result\": \"Fetched May 2026 euro area unemployment rate 6.2%, April 2026 6.2%, May 2025 6.3%, and EU May 2026 5.9%.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1118:    \"question\": \"BEA Personal Income and Outlays: PCE price index excluding food and energy, seasonally adjusted percent change from preceding month, June 2026, first print\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1127:    \"resolutionRule\": \"Resolve to the first BEA Personal Income and Outlays print for June 2026 PCE price index excluding food and energy, seasonally adjusted percent change from the preceding month, released July 30, 2026 at 8:30 a.m. EDT. Use the first published value at BEA's reported precision; ignore later revisions or benchmark updates.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1130:        \"label\": \"May 2026 BEA core PCE MoM first print\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1134:        \"label\": \"April 2026 BEA core PCE MoM first print\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1138:        \"label\": \"March 2026 BEA core PCE MoM first print\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1142:        \"label\": \"February 2026 BEA core PCE MoM first print\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1146:        \"label\": \"January 2026 BEA core PCE MoM first print\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1150:        \"label\": \"May 2026 BEA core PCE YoY\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1159:      \"No June BEA PCE source data were yet available at run time\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1280:        \"text\": \"The target is the first BEA Personal Income and Outlays print for June 2026: the PCE price index excluding food and energy, seasonally adjusted percent change from the prior month. The BEA release and data page are the resolver; later revised tables do not change this first-print target.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1285:        \"call\": \"BEA 2026 release schedule for Personal Income and Outlays, June 2026\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1291:        \"call\": \"BEA core PCE data page for current release status and 12-month reference class\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1292:        \"result\": \"Fetched BEA data page: May 2026 core PCE 12-month change was 3.4%, April 2026 was 3.3%, March 2026 was 3.3%, February 2026 was 3.0%; current release date was June 25, 2026 and next release date was July 30, 2026.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1297:        \"call\": \"BEA May 2026 Personal Income and Outlays release\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1303:        \"call\": \"BEA January through April 2026 Personal Income and Outlays releases\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1304:        \"result\": \"Fetched recent BEA monthly prints: January 2026 core PCE rose 0.4%, February 2026 core PCE rose 0.4%, March 2026 core PCE rose 0.3%, and April 2026 first-print core PCE rose 0.2%; headline PCE rose 0.3% in January, 0.4% in February, 0.7% in March, and 0.4% in April.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1308:        \"text\": \"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: the official BEA monthly core PCE prints available for January through May 2026 are 0.4, 0.4, 0.3, 0.2, and 0.3. Their simple mean is 0.32, while latest-print persistence is 0.30, so the base rate centers just above 0.30 before June-specific adjustments.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1316:        \"text\": \"Prior/update/interval: persistence prior = 0.30 from the May 2026 BEA first print; historical sample = Jan-May BEA monthly core PCE values 0.4, 0.4, 0.3, 0.2, 0.3 with mean 0.32; adjustment components = +0.02 for the elevated 3.4% year-over-year core level and still-firm services inflation, -0.01 for the April-May stabilization and no June source-data confirmation yet; point = 0.31. Interval method = realized recent first-print dispersion plus release-rounding risk: recent values span 0.2 to 0.4, so an 80% band of about +/-0.15 around 0.31 gives 0.16 to 0.47.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1795:        \"label\": \"June 2026 unemployment rate, percent\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1802:      \"The unemployment rate remained low at 4.2 percent and unemployed people were 7.1 million, limiting the case for a sharp vacancy collapse.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1945:        \"result\": \"Fetched June 2026 labor indicators: nonfarm payroll employment +57,000; unemployment rate 4.2 percent; unemployed people 7.1 million; labor force participation 61.5 percent; April-May payroll revisions totaled -74,000.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1957:        \"text\": \"Current-release adjustment: June payroll growth of +57,000 and -74,000 in April-May revisions point to softer labor demand, so I subtract about 0.14 to 0.15 million from pure May persistence. The 4.2 percent unemployment rate and 7.1 million unemployed count keep the adjustment moderate rather than recession-sized.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1980:    \"question\": \"What will be the first-published seasonally adjusted U.S. civilian unemployment rate (U-3) for July 2026, in percent, in the BLS Employment Situation release?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1989:    \"resolutionRule\": \"Resolves to the first-published seasonally adjusted civilian unemployment rate (U-3) for July 2026 stated in the BLS Employment Situation news release scheduled for August 7, 2026 at 08:30 AM ET. Use the rate rounded to one decimal percent as first published by BLS; later revisions or annual population-control changes do not change the resolved value.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1992:        \"label\": \"Feb 2026 SA unemployment rate\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1996:        \"label\": \"Mar 2026 SA unemployment rate\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2000:        \"label\": \"Apr 2026 SA unemployment rate\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2004:        \"label\": \"May 2026 SA unemployment rate\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2008:        \"label\": \"Jun 2026 SA unemployment rate\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2130:        \"text\": \"Forecast for July 2026 U.S. unemployment rate first print\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2134:        \"text\": \"Framing and exact resolver: this is the BLS Current Population Survey seasonally adjusted civilian unemployment rate, U-3, for July 2026, as first printed in the Employment Situation release. The ledger target slug unemployment-rate-july-2026 is consistent with a July reference month and August 2026 BLS release date.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2146:        \"result\": \"Fetched BLS June 2026 summary values: unemployment rate 4.2 percent, unemployed people 7.1 million, total nonfarm payroll employment +57,000, labor force participation rate 61.5 percent, employment-population ratio 59.0 percent.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2152:        \"result\": \"Fetched seasonally adjusted total unemployment rates from Table A-1: Feb. 2026 = 4.4 percent, Mar. 2026 = 4.3 percent, Apr. 2026 = 4.3 percent, May 2026 = 4.3 percent, Jun. 2026 = 4.2 percent; unemployed levels were 7.571 million in Feb. 2026 and 7.094 million in Jun. 2026.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2164:        \"text\": \"Prior/update/interval: prior model is rounded latest-value persistence with a five-month BLS first-print sample of Feb-Jun 2026 unemployment rates at 4.4, 4.3, 4.3, 4.3, and 4.2. I start at latest 4.2, add +0.05 pp because the June improvement was helped by lower participation, add +0.04 pp for weak June payroll momentum, and add +0.01 pp for mean reversion toward the five-month average near 4.3. The interval method uses recent one-month rounded-rate dispersion, where typical moves are 0.0 to 0.1 pp, then widens to +/-0.2 pp for CPS sampling and labor-force participation uncertainty, giving final implied bounds of 4.1 to 4.5 around a 4.3 rounded point.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2357:        \"result\": \"The June 2026 release reported total nonfarm payroll employment +57,000, unemployment rate 4.2 percent, unemployed people 7.1 million, prior-12-month average payroll change +36,000, April revised from +179,000 to +148,000, May revised from +172,000 to +129,000, and the July 2026 release scheduled for Aug. 07, 2026.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2375:        \"result\": \"For the week ending June 27, 2026, seasonally adjusted initial claims were 215,000, down 1,000 from 216,000; the 4-week average was 222,000; insured unemployment for the week ending June 20 was 1,814,000 and the insured unemployment rate was 1.2 percent.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2383:        \"text\": \"Level, momentum, one-off, and policy split: the June +57 thousand print and downward revisions point to slower hiring, but claims at 215 thousand and a 1.2 percent insured unemployment rate do not show a layoff shock. June leisure and hospitality employment fell by 61 thousand in the BLS summary, which could partly reverse in July, while no policy mechanism directly forces a large July payroll discontinuity.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t23-31-12z-statbel-cpi-headline-yoy-2026-07/raw_response.txt:1:{\"slug\":\"belgium-cpi-annual-rate-july-2026\",\"country\":\"EA\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"Belgium CPI inflation, July 2026\",\"question\":\"Statbel Belgian headline consumer price index annual inflation rate for July 2026, not seasonally adjusted, first official print\",\"unit\":\"percent\",\"pointEstimate\":3.2,\"ciLow\":2.4,\"ciHigh\":4.1,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-07-30\",\"resolutionSource\":\"Statbel Consumer Price Index - Health Index release\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://statbel.fgov.be/en/themes/consumer-prices/consumer-price-index\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolve to the first Statbel-published annual inflation rate for the Belgian headline Consumer Price Index for m-2026-07, reported in percent and rounded to the agency's published precision; do not use later revisions or mirrors.\",\"dataPointId\":\"statbel.cpi.headline_yoy.2026-07.first_print\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"2026-06 headline CPI inflation\",\"value\":3.4},{\"label\":\"2026-05 headline CPI inflation\",\"value\":4.08},{\"label\":\"2026-04 headline CPI inflation\",\"value\":4.01},{\"label\":\"2026-03 headline CPI inflation\",\"value\":1.65},{\"label\":\"2025-07 headline CPI inflation\",\"value\":1.92}],\"drivers\":[\"June headline inflation fell from May but remained above the 13-month average\",\"Energy inflation was still positive but eased in June\",\"Food inflation was near zero and pulls down the July base case\",\"Services and rents remain sticky upside pressure\",\"July travel and holiday components can add seasonal volatility\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://statbel.fgov.be/en/themes/consumer-prices/consumer-price-index\",\"https://statbel.fgov.be/en/calendar\",\"https://bestat.statbel.fgov.be/bestat/crosstable.xhtml?view=31b8021d-3fcd-4a8b-ac49-92beabf434e9\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-07-04T23:31:20Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"Belgium July 2026 Headline CPI Inflation Forecast\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The resolver is the first Statbel print for Belgian headline CPI annual inflation for July 2026, not a HICP flash estimate and not a revised database value. Statbel defines inflation as the ratio of the CPI for a month to the CPI for the same month one year earlier.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked Statbel release calendar for the target month and publication date.\",\"result\":\"The official calendar lists Consumer Price Index - Health Index for m-2026-07 on 30 July 2026.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Read the latest Statbel CPI release page for recent headline CPI inflation and index levels.\",\"result\":\"For June 2026, Statbel reported headline inflation at 3.40%, May at 4.08%, April at 4.01%, March at 1.65%, CPI index 102.95 in June, and CPI index 103.26 in May.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Read the latest Statbel release details for components and monthly movements.\",\"result\":\"June 2026 CPI fell by 0.31 points or 0.30% month on month; health-index inflation was 2.99%, core inflation was 3.04%, energy inflation was 10.31%, food inflation was 0.06%, services inflation was 5.10%, and rent inflation was 3.38%.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Read the official be.STAT table for the 13-month annual-inflation reference class.\",\"result\":\"The be.STAT last-13-month global headline inflation values include July 2025 at 1.92%, January 2026 at 1.10%, April 2026 at 4.01%, May 2026 at 4.08%, and June 2026 at 3.40%.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The base rate/reference class from the last 13 official monthly headline prints averages about 2.33%, but the latest 3 prints average about 3.83%. I weight the near-term state more heavily because the April-June regime includes the current energy and services configuration, while keeping the longer reference class as a pull toward lower inflation.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Prior/update/interval: Start with a June persistence prior of 3.40% and a latest-3-month state anchor of 3.83% against a 13-month historical sample mean of 2.33%; update by -0.25 pp for easing energy from 11.20% in May to 10.31% in June and by -0.10 pp for food at 0.06%, offset by +0.10 pp for services at 5.10%, rents at 3.38%, and July travel seasonality. Rounded point = 3.20%. The 80% interval uses recent first-print monthly volatility around persistence, widened for July energy/travel swings, giving 2.40% to 4.10%.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Upside risk: renewed electricity, gas, motor-fuel, air-ticket, or package-holiday increases could keep the annual rate near May's 4.08% or would land above the interval if they combine with stronger services. Downside risk: another June-like monthly CPI decline, weaker fuel prices, or broader goods discounts could push the print below 2.40% outside the interval.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":3.2,\"ciLow\":2.4,\"ciHigh\":4.1}]}\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:19:    \"BEA personal income and outlays\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:20:    \"BEA gross domestic product\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:679:      \"BEA Table 2.6, Treasury outlay timing, and benefit-program caseload trends\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:706:          \"What will BEA first report as government social benefits to persons in May 2026, seasonally adjusted annual rate?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:714:          \"Resolves to the first published May 2026 value for government social benefits to persons in BEA NIPA Table 2.6, line 17, converted from millions to billions of current dollars at a seasonally adjusted annual rate. Later revisions do not change the resolved value.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:745:      \"BEA Table 2.6 line 18, SSA benefit rolls, COLA, and Treasury benefit-payment timing\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:772:          \"What will BEA first report as Social Security government social benefits to persons in May 2026, seasonally adjusted annual rate?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:780:          \"Resolves to the first published May 2026 value for Social Security in BEA NIPA Table 2.6, line 18, converted from millions to billions of current dollars at a seasonally adjusted annual rate. Later revisions do not change the resolved value.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:811:      \"BEA Table 2.6 line 19, CMS enrollment, Medicare Advantage payments, and provider payment updates\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:838:          \"What will BEA first report as Medicare government social benefits to persons in May 2026, seasonally adjusted annual rate?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:846:          \"Resolves to the first published May 2026 value for Medicare in BEA NIPA Table 2.6, line 19, converted from millions to billions of current dollars at a seasonally adjusted annual rate. Later revisions do not change the resolved value.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:877:      \"BEA Table 2.6 line 20, CMS Medicaid enrollment, FMAP, and state payment timing\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:880:      \"Medicaid is the cleanest near-term BEA bridge from public benefit rules and enrollment operations into official household income accounts.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:904:          \"What will BEA first report as Medicaid government social benefits to persons in May 2026, seasonally adjusted annual rate?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:912:          \"Resolves to the first published May 2026 value for Medicaid in BEA NIPA Table 2.6, line 20, converted from millions to billions of current dollars at a seasonally adjusted annual rate. Later revisions do not change the resolved value.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:925:            call: 'agent.ensemble({ task: \"forecast May 2026 BEA Medicaid benefits\", independent_agents: 2 })',\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:948:      \"BEA Table 2.6 line 3, BLS payroll employment, average hourly earnings, and hours\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:975:          \"What will BEA first report as wages and salaries in May 2026, seasonally adjusted annual rate?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:982:          \"Resolves to the first published May 2026 value for wages and salaries in BEA NIPA Table 2.6, line 3, converted from millions to billions of current dollars at a seasonally adjusted annual rate. Later revisions do not change the resolved value.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:1018:      \"BEA Table 2.6 line 26, Treasury individual receipts, payroll income, and withheld-tax timing\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:1049:          \"What will BEA first report as personal current taxes in May 2026, seasonally adjusted annual rate?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:1056:          \"Resolves to the first published May 2026 value for less personal current taxes in BEA NIPA Table 2.6, line 26, converted from millions to billions of current dollars at a seasonally adjusted annual rate. Later revisions do not change the resolved value.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:1069:            call: 'agent.ensemble({ task: \"forecast May 2026 BEA personal current taxes\", independent_agents: 2 })',\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:1075:          \"Two independent agents centered near $3,267 billion SAAR after comparing April's $3,250.3 billion first-print level with recent April-to-May tax increments, Jan-Apr year-over-year growth, and the resolved May payroll report. The ensemble keeps a wider right tail for withholding, refund, and nonwithheld-payment timing around the first BEA estimate.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:1092:      \"BEA Table 2.6 line 27, personal income, taxes, wages, and government transfers\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:1119:          \"What will BEA first report as disposable personal income in May 2026, seasonally adjusted annual rate?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:1127:          \"Resolves to the first published May 2026 value for disposable personal income in BEA NIPA Table 2.6, line 27, converted from millions to billions of current dollars at a seasonally adjusted annual rate. Later revisions do not change the resolved value.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:1180:          \"What will BEA first report as the monthly percent change in the PCE price index for May 2026?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:1187:          \"Resolves to the first published BEA monthly percent change in the PCE price index for May 2026 in the Personal Income and Outlays release. Later revisions do not change the resolved value.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:1215:    benchmark: \"BEA second estimate, source-data revisions, and GDP nowcasts\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:1241:          \"What will BEA first report as real GDP growth in the third estimate for Q1 2026, seasonally adjusted annual rate?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:1248:          \"Resolves to the first published BEA third estimate of real GDP percent change for Q1 2026, seasonally adjusted annual rate. Later annual-update or benchmark revisions do not change the resolved value.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t23-31-12z-statbel-cpi-headline-yoy-2026-07/normalized_cells.json:6:  \"title\": \"Belgium CPI inflation, July 2026\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t23-31-12z-statbel-cpi-headline-yoy-2026-07/normalized_cells.json:56:    \"text\": \"Belgium July 2026 Headline CPI Inflation Forecast\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/batches/2026-07-04/july-roll.json:161:        \"series\": \"eurostat.unemployment_rate\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t23-31-12z-statbel-cpi-headline-yoy-2026-07/manifest.json:9:    \"country\": \"BE\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-07-05z-bls-ces-nonfarm-payrolls-change-2026-07/raw_response.txt:1:{\"slug\":\"nonfarm-payrolls-july-2026\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"US nonfarm payrolls, Jul 2026\",\"question\":\"What will be the first-published seasonally adjusted over-the-month change in U.S. total nonfarm payroll employment for July 2026, in thousands, in the BLS Employment Situation release?\",\"unit\":\"thousands\",\"pointEstimate\":85,\"ciLow\":-65,\"ciHigh\":235,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-08-07\",\"resolutionSource\":\"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Employment Situation (Current Employment Statistics)\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolves to the first-published seasonally adjusted over-the-month change in total nonfarm payroll employment for July 2026 stated in the BLS Employment Situation news release scheduled for August 7, 2026. Use the BLS first print in thousands as released; later monthly revisions, annual benchmark revisions, and subsequent table updates do not change the resolved value.\",\"dataPointId\":\"bls.ces.total_nonfarm_payroll_change.july_2026.first_print\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"June 2026 first print payroll change, thousands\",\"value\":57},{\"label\":\"May 2026 first print payroll change, thousands\",\"value\":172},{\"label\":\"April 2026 first print payroll change, thousands\",\"value\":115},{\"label\":\"March 2026 first print payroll change, thousands\",\"value\":178},{\"label\":\"February 2026 first print payroll change, thousands\",\"value\":-92}],\"drivers\":[\"June print was soft but not a collapse\",\"recent first prints average near moderate job growth\",\"claims remain low by recent standards\",\"health care and social assistance provide steady payroll support\",\"leisure and hospitality June weakness could partly reverse\",\"prior-month downward revisions warn against over-weighting spring strength\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://www.bls.gov/schedule/news_release/empsit.htm\",\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm\",\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.b.htm\",\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_06052026.htm\",\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_04032026.htm\",\"https://www.dol.gov/ui/data.pdf\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-07-04T19:07:32Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"July 2026 first-print nonfarm payroll forecast\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The resolver is the first BLS Employment Situation print for July 2026 total nonfarm payroll employment, seasonally adjusted, measured as the over-the-month change in thousands. I found no local registered July ledger row; I kept the canonical requested slug and used the existing BLS payroll ledger pattern for the dataPointId and first-print resolver.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the official BLS Employment Situation release calendar for the July 2026 reference month.\",\"result\":\"BLS lists July 2026 Employment Situation release on Aug. 07, 2026 at 08:30 AM; the same schedule lists June 2026 on Jul. 02, 2026 and May 2026 on Jun. 05, 2026.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Read the current BLS Employment Situation Summary for June 2026 payroll level, unemployment, revisions, and next-release notice.\",\"result\":\"The June 2026 release reported total nonfarm payroll employment +57,000, unemployment rate 4.2 percent, prior-12-month average payroll change +36,000, April revised from +179,000 to +148,000, May revised from +172,000 to +129,000, and the July 2026 release scheduled for Aug. 07, 2026.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Read BLS Summary table B for recent establishment-survey composition.\",\"result\":\"Summary table B shows total nonfarm changes of +148,000 in April 2026, +129,000 in May 2026, and +57,000 in June 2026; the 3-month average was +111,000; June sector changes included professional and business services +36,000, private education and health services +69,000, leisure and hospitality -61,000, and government +8,000.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Read BLS archived Employment Situation releases for recent first-print payroll reference points.\",\"result\":\"The May 2026 first print was +172,000; the May release stated April had first printed at +115,000 before revision to +179,000; the March 2026 release first printed March at +178,000 and February at -133,000 after February had first printed at -92,000.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Read the latest official DOL unemployment insurance weekly claims release for near-term layoff pressure.\",\"result\":\"For the week ending June 27, 2026, seasonally adjusted initial claims were 215,000, down 1,000 from 216,000; the 4-week average was 222,000; insured unemployment for the week ending June 20 was 1,814,000 and the insured unemployment rate was 1.2 percent.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: the recent first-print payroll sample of February through June is -92, +178, +115, +172, and +57 thousand, averaging about +86 thousand. The current BLS revised-data frame is softer, with +36 thousand over the prior 12 months but +111 thousand over the latest three revised months.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Level, momentum, and one-off split: June's +57 thousand print and downward revisions argue for a lower center than the spring first prints. The claims data do not show a layoff break, and the June leisure and hospitality -61 thousand looks like a candidate for partial reversal, so I do not push the center toward zero or negative payroll growth.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Policy-mechanism and sector split: health care, social assistance, and some professional services remain the reliable upside channels, while restrictive rates, slower consumer demand, weak participation, and government-sector cooling limit acceleration. July seasonal payroll measurement can also be noisy around education, hospitality, and temporary-help hiring.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Prior/update/interval: prior model is a blended persistence prior using the recent official first-print sample from February-June 2026 and the BLS current-release 12-month and 3-month reference averages. I start near the +86 thousand first-print average, subtract about 20 thousand for June softness and the -74 thousand combined April-May revision signal, add about 15 thousand for likely partial leisure/hospitality rebound and low claims, and round to +85 thousand. The 80% interval uses realized recent first-print dispersion of roughly 100 thousand, widened for July seasonal risk and skewed slightly upward for rebound risk, giving -65 to +235 thousand.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Point calculation: 86 - 20 + 15 = 81, rounded to 85 thousand to avoid false precision. Interval calculation: center 85 with a lower half-width of 150 and upper half-width of 150 gives -65 to 235 thousand, calibrated wider than one recent standard deviation because first prints can swing sharply and July seasonal factors add noise.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration and scenarios: downside outside the interval would require a broader hiring freeze, a larger leisure or retail drop, or an abrupt claims deterioration pushing payrolls below -65 thousand. Upside outside the interval would require a strong rebound from June's leisure weakness plus continued health-care and professional-services gains above 235 thousand. The central case is modest positive hiring, slower than spring but not recessionary.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":85,\"ciLow\":-65,\"ciHigh\":235}]}\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-13-54z-bea-core-pce-mom-2026-06/raw_response.txt:1:{\"slug\":\"us-core-pce-mom-june-2026\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"US core PCE MoM June 2026\",\"question\":\"BEA Personal Income and Outlays: PCE price index excluding food and energy, seasonally adjusted percent change from preceding month, June 2026, first print\",\"unit\":\"percent_growth\",\"pointEstimate\":0.31,\"ciLow\":0.16,\"ciHigh\":0.47,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-07-30\",\"resolutionSource\":\"U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis, Personal Income and Outlays\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://www.bea.gov/data/personal-consumption-expenditures-price-index-excluding-food-and-energy\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolve to the first BEA Personal Income and Outlays print for June 2026 PCE price index excluding food and energy, seasonally adjusted percent change from the preceding month, released July 30, 2026 at 8:30 a.m. EDT. Use the first published value at BEA's reported precision; ignore later revisions or benchmark updates.\",\"dataPointId\":\"us.bea.core_pce.mom_sa.2026-06\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"May 2026 BEA core PCE MoM first print\",\"value\":0.3},{\"label\":\"April 2026 BEA core PCE MoM as shown in May release\",\"value\":0.3},{\"label\":\"March 2026 BEA core PCE MoM first print\",\"value\":0.3},{\"label\":\"February 2026 BEA core PCE MoM first print\",\"value\":0.4},{\"label\":\"January 2026 BEA core PCE MoM first print\",\"value\":0.4},{\"label\":\"May 2026 BEA core PCE YoY\",\"value\":3.4}],\"drivers\":[\"Recent core PCE persistence is clustered around 0.3 percent\",\"Core CPI cooled to 0.2 percent in May while core services remained firm\",\"PPI services and core final demand were still elevated in May\",\"Energy shock mostly affects headline PCE, but some indirect pass-through risk remains\",\"No June CPI or PPI prints were available at run time\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://www.bea.gov/news/schedule\",\"https://www.bea.gov/data/personal-consumption-expenditures-price-index-excluding-food-and-energy\",\"https://www.bea.gov/news/2026/personal-income-and-outlays-may-2026\",\"https://www.bea.gov/news/2026/personal-income-and-outlays-april-2026\",\"https://www.bea.gov/news/2026/personal-income-and-outlays-march-2026\",\"https://www.bea.gov/news/2026/personal-income-and-outlays-february-2026\",\"https://www.bea.gov/news/2026/personal-income-and-outlays-january-2026\",\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/cpi_06102026.htm\",\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/ppi_06112026.htm\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-07-04T19:14:20Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"Forecast June 2026 US core PCE monthly inflation\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The target is the first BEA Personal Income and Outlays print for June 2026: PCE price index excluding food and energy, seasonally adjusted percent change from the prior month. The BEA release page, not FRED or later revised tables, is the resolving source.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"BEA 2026 release schedule for Personal Income and Outlays, June 2026\",\"result\":\"Fetched official schedule: Personal Income and Outlays, June 2026 is listed for July 30, 2026 at 8:30 AM; the same schedule also lists Personal Income and Outlays, July 2026 for August 26, 2026 at 8:30 AM.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"BEA core PCE data page and May 2026 Personal Income and Outlays release\",\"result\":\"Fetched BEA current context: core PCE 12-month change was 3.4% in May 2026, 3.3% in April 2026, 3.3% in March 2026, and 3.0% in February 2026; the current release date was June 25, 2026 and the next release date was July 30, 2026. May 2026 core PCE increased 0.3% month over month, headline PCE increased 0.4%, April core PCE in the May table was 0.3%, and May real PCE rose 0.3%.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"BEA January through April 2026 Personal Income and Outlays releases\",\"result\":\"Fetched recent BEA monthly prints: January 2026 core PCE rose 0.4%, February 2026 core PCE rose 0.4%, March 2026 core PCE rose 0.3%, and April 2026 first print core PCE rose 0.2%; headline PCE rose 0.3% in January, 0.4% in February, 0.7% in March, and 0.4% in April.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"BLS May 2026 CPI and PPI releases for public source-price context before June PCE inputs\",\"result\":\"Fetched BLS May 2026 context: CPI-U rose 0.5%, core CPI rose 0.2%, shelter rose 0.3%, services less energy services rose 0.3%, PPI final demand rose 1.1%, final demand services rose 0.3%, final demand less foods, energy, and trade rose 0.8%, and PPI final demand goods less foods and energy rose 0.8%.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: the official BEA recent monthly core PCE prints available this run are 0.4, 0.4, 0.3, 0.2, and 0.3 for January through May 2026, with a simple mean of 0.32 and a latest-print persistence prior of 0.30. The outside-view center is therefore just above 0.30, before June-specific information.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Level, momentum, one-off, and policy-mechanism split: the 12-month core PCE rate rose to 3.4%, so the level is still too high for a quick return to 0.2 prints. Momentum is stable around 0.3 after April-May. Energy is a headline shock, but PPI and transportation inputs create some indirect upside risk; cooling May core CPI and softer motor-vehicle prices cap the upside.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Prior/update/interval: persistence prior = 0.30 from the May 2026 BEA first print; historical sample = Jan-May BEA monthly core PCE values 0.4, 0.4, 0.3, 0.2, 0.3 with mean 0.32; adjustment components = +0.02 for elevated 3.4% year-over-year core PCE and firm PPI services/core final demand, -0.01 for May core CPI cooling to 0.2 and no June CPI/PPI yet; point = 0.31. Interval method = realized recent first-print dispersion plus rounding risk: recent values span 0.2 to 0.4, so use an 80% band of about +/-0.15 around 0.31, yielding 0.16 to 0.47.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration and tails: downside outside the interval would require a June CPI/PPI mix with broad goods deflation, softer medical/financial services, and shelter below recent 0.3 readings, pushing core PCE below 0.16. Upside outside the interval would require energy pass-through plus another portfolio-management, airfares, or transportation-services jump like the May PPI details, pushing core PCE above 0.47. The central case is persistence near 0.3 with modest upside pressure.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":0.31,\"ciLow\":0.16,\"ciHigh\":0.47}]}\nrecords/thesis-analyst/batches/2026-07-04/belgium-wave.json:16:        \"country\": \"BE\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/batches/2026-07-04/belgium-wave.json:33:        \"country\": \"BE\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-07-05z-bls-ces-nonfarm-payrolls-change-2026-07/normalized_cells.js