nemployment-rate-may-2026-first-print.2026-06-08T00-00-00-02-00.1\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2150973:      \"runId\": \"run.unemployment-rate-may-2026-first-print.2026-06-08T00-00-00-02-00\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2150976:      \"publicText\": \"The unemployment rate pairs with payrolls but has different noise: household-survey employment, labor-force entry, and rounding to one decimal place. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 week lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, +12 months, threshold questions.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2150980:      \"reasoningEventId\": \"reasoning.run.unemployment-rate-may-2026-first-print.2026-06-08T00-00-00-02-00.2\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2150981:      \"runId\": \"run.unemployment-rate-may-2026-first-print.2026-06-08T00-00-00-02-00\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2150984:      \"publicText\": \"The unemployment rate has held at 4.3% for two consecutive months after 4.4% in February. Claims and insured unemployment do not yet point to a sharp break, so the run keeps the center unchanged with a two-tenths 80% interval.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2150988:      \"reasoningEventId\": \"reasoning.run.unemployment-rate-may-2026-first-print.2026-06-08T00-00-00-02-00.3\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2150989:      \"runId\": \"run.unemployment-rate-may-2026-first-print.2026-06-08T00-00-00-02-00\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2151432:      \"publicText\": \"Average hourly earnings connects labor-market tightness to household income and price pressure, and resolves on the same schedule as payrolls and unemployment. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 day lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, threshold questions.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2151592:      \"publicText\": \"Level and momentum effects point in opposite directions. April's 7.618 million level is high relative to January through March, but May payrolls were solid and unemployment was unchanged, so I expect only a partial reversal of the April jump rather than a fall back to the March trough.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152364:      \"reasoningEventId\": \"reasoning.run.uk-unemployment-rate-feb-apr-2026.2026-06-04T10-32-04-01-00.0\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152365:      \"runId\": \"run.uk-unemployment-rate-feb-apr-2026.2026-06-04T10-32-04-01-00\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152372:      \"reasoningEventId\": \"reasoning.run.uk-unemployment-rate-feb-apr-2026.2026-06-04T10-32-04-01-00.1\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152373:      \"runId\": \"run.uk-unemployment-rate-feb-apr-2026.2026-06-04T10-32-04-01-00\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152376:      \"publicText\": \"The UK labour market is currently one of the clearest stress points: unemployment is up, payroll employment is falling, and wage growth remains central to Bank of England decisions. This target resolves on 2026-06-18 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~2 weeks lag. The same series can also spawn next release, quarterly path, claimant threshold questions.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152380:      \"reasoningEventId\": \"reasoning.run.uk-unemployment-rate-feb-apr-2026.2026-06-04T10-32-04-01-00.2\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152381:      \"runId\": \"run.uk-unemployment-rate-feb-apr-2026.2026-06-04T10-32-04-01-00\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152384:      \"publicText\": \"The January-March unemployment rate was 5.0%, but the provisional April PAYE estimate fell by 100,000 on the month and vacancies reached their lowest level since early 2021. The agent nudges the rate up while respecting LFS sampling volatility.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152388:      \"reasoningEventId\": \"reasoning.run.uk-unemployment-rate-feb-apr-2026.2026-06-04T10-32-04-01-00.3\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152389:      \"runId\": \"run.uk-unemployment-rate-feb-apr-2026.2026-06-04T10-32-04-01-00\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152396:      \"reasoningEventId\": \"reasoning.run.uk-unemployment-rate-apr-jun-2026.2026-06-04T10-32-04-01-00.0\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152397:      \"runId\": \"run.uk-unemployment-rate-apr-jun-2026.2026-06-04T10-32-04-01-00\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152404:      \"reasoningEventId\": \"reasoning.run.uk-unemployment-rate-apr-jun-2026.2026-06-04T10-32-04-01-00.1\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152405:      \"runId\": \"run.uk-unemployment-rate-apr-jun-2026.2026-06-04T10-32-04-01-00\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152408:      \"publicText\": \"The UK labour market is currently one of the clearest stress points: unemployment is up, payroll employment is falling, and wage growth remains central to Bank of England decisions. This target resolves on 2026-08-18 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~2 weeks lag. The same series can also spawn next release, quarterly path, claimant threshold questions.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152412:      \"reasoningEventId\": \"reasoning.run.uk-unemployment-rate-apr-jun-2026.2026-06-04T10-32-04-01-00.2\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152413:      \"runId\": \"run.uk-unemployment-rate-apr-jun-2026.2026-06-04T10-32-04-01-00\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152416:  