026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:84:    \"call\": \"Checked BLS Table A-1 for the latest seasonally adjusted monthly unemployment-rate path.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:85:    \"result\": \"Seasonally adjusted unemployment rates were Jan. 2026 4.3, Feb. 2026 4.4, Mar. 2026 4.3, Apr. 2026 4.3, May 2026 4.3.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:89:    \"text\": \"Base-rate/reference-class step: one-month changes in the rounded unemployment rate are usually small, and the last five official monthly prints have stayed in a 4.3 to 4.4 range, with four of five at 4.3.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:93:    \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: household-survey noise can move the rounded unemployment rate by 0.1 or 0.2 points even when payroll growth is solid, and long-term unemployment near 2.0 million leaves some upside risk.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-17-15z-bls-jolts-job-openings-2026-05/cells.with_activity.json:42:      \"May payroll growth was solid at 172,000 and unemployment held at 4.3 percent\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-17-15z-bls-jolts-job-openings-2026-05/cells.with_activity.json:84:        \"result\": \"BLS reported May 2026 payroll employment +172,000, unemployment rate 4.3 percent, April payroll revision to +179,000, and March payroll revision to +214,000.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-17-15z-bls-jolts-job-openings-2026-05/cells.with_activity.json:92:        \"text\": \"The upside case is that solid May payroll growth and stable unemployment mean labor demand remained firm, so April's 7,618 thousand could persist into May.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-11-48z-us-dol-initial-claims-sa-week-